{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. He was involved in the politics of the Crusader states and the wider Muslim-Christian conflicts.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early life and accession**\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was killed by the Hashashin a sect of Nizari Ismailis. Hodierna of Rethel his mother took control of Tripoli as regent. Raymond III was raised in a politically charged environment. He was educated and prepared for rulership from a young age. He took full control of Tripoli in 1174 after Hodierna stepped aside.\n\n**Political and military career**\nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Levant. He allied with Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler who sought to unite Muslim forces against the Crusaders. This alliance was controversial among Christian factions. He also mediated disputes between Bohemond III of Antioch and the Knights Templar. Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Dins forces in 1164 after the Battle of Harim. He was held prisoner for nearly ten years until a ransom was paid. This captivity weakened his control over Tripoli temporarily.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Conflict with Saladin and later years**\nRaymond III returned to Tripoli in 1174 after his release. He faced growing threats from Saladin who sought to conquer the Crusader states. In 1180 he married Eschiva of Bures Princess of Galilee to strengthen alliances. He supported Guy of Lusignan as king of Jerusalem though tensions existed. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly before Saladins decisive victory at the Battle of Hattin. His death marked the end of an era for Tripoli as it faced increased pressure from Muslim forces.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III is remembered as a pragmatic ruler who navigated complex political landscapes. His alliances with Muslim leaders were controversial but aimed at preserving Christian territories. He contributed to the defense of Tripoli during a turbulent period. His marriage to Eschiva strengthened ties between key Crusader factions. His death coincided with the decline of Crusader power in the region. Raymond IIIs life reflects the intricate power struggles of the time.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.44731006026268005}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -7.879423355916515e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.029690705239772797}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -8.308542601298541e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.9141146923357155e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4918982982635498}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.2963669002056122}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0002232540718978271}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.16735891997814178}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.2294001579284668}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.437280717364047e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009613183210603893}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0004807746736332774}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6093124941107817e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00013529339048545808}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0005245024804025888}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.48860906646587e-05}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.34854763746261597}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.007631788495928049}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.1219027985353023e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.731181277544238e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.671578815556131e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -2.8013